## 1. Vision and product summary

### 1.1 Product summary

We are building a multi-tenant platform that lets small made-to-order businesses run their whole operation, from taking an order to payment and collection, without building their own software. Each business configures its own workflow, branding and web address on shared infrastructure. (Product name: **Ojúòwò**, a Yoruba name meaning the face of the business, subject to a domain, trademark and company-name check. Web addresses use plain letters, for example `ojuowo`, because addresses cannot carry accent marks. This document says "the platform".)

**The solution.** One source of truth for orders, staff, customers and money. Staff see only the work assigned to them and update its progress. Customers see their order moving through stages and see what they owe. Payments are recorded by the business. The owner sees everything.

### 1.2 Problem statement

Businesses such as tailors, barbing salons, shoe makers and farms run on notebooks, WhatsApp chats and memory. Orders get lost, staff pay is disputed, customers keep asking "is it ready?", and the owner cannot easily see who has paid and who has not.

**Evidence (to be filled before design sign-off).**
- [N] interviews with [N] businesses, including S.I.G Clothing Company.
- [N] hours per week the owner and staff spend chasing orders, payments and wage counts today.
- [N] orders lost or late in the last 3 months, and [N] wage disputes.

The S.I.G proposal and interview notes are attached in §19.

### 1.3 Goals and non-goals

**Goals**
1. Let one tailoring business run its full order cycle on the platform, daily (Phase 1).
2. Make staff pay accurate and disputes rare. Each worker is paid a set amount for each approved piece of work (for example, a set price for each agbada sewn), and every task is recorded.
3. Let customers see progress and their balance without calling the business.
4. Turn the tailoring product into a platform for other stage-based businesses (Phase 2).
5. Connect businesses to their suppliers and pay staff through the platform (Phase 3).

**Non-goals (for the whole product, until stated otherwise)**
- Not accounting software: no ledger, tax filing or bank reconciliation.
- Not a marketplace of strangers in Phases 1 to 3. Supplier connections are invite-only.
- No AI features in Phases 1 to 3 (see §13).
- No support for businesses that sell by planting or harvest planning (farm planning). Farms are supported only as businesses that sell produce by customer order.
- No sales tax or discounts on invoices in Phase 1. The manager enters the final price.
- **Customers do not pay through the platform in Phase 1.** The tracking link shows progress and balance only. The manager records cash and transfer payments.
- **Subscription billing** starts in Phase 2A. The business wallet (for subscription payments) is a Phase 2A addition; Phase 1 uses Paga only for staff payouts.

### 1.4 The launch wedge

Phase 1 is built for tailoring and clothing businesses, using the S.I.G Clothing Company proposal as the reference customer. The underlying model is generic from day one (an Order with Items that move through configurable stages), so later phases can onboard other **stage-based, made-to-order** businesses (shoe making, laundry, bakery orders, farms selling produce by order) by configuration.

Businesses that run on time slots (barbing salons) need the **Appointments** module in Phase 2. That module is new work and is not promised by configuration alone.

**Technical rule that affects design.** Stages are stored per business, not hard-coded. Order details are stored as field definitions per business type, not as tailoring-only columns. This keeps Phase 2 from requiring a data migration.

### 1.5 Roadmap and gates

Phase 1 is designed first. Each gate is the real-world outcome that shows a phase has worked. The gates are written out in §16.

