## 3. Core concepts the designer must understand

The platform works for different businesses because every business is described with the same building blocks. Learn these and the rest of the document follows.

| Concept | Plain meaning | Tailoring example | Barbing salon example (Phase 2) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Business (tenant) | One company on the platform, with its own data, branding and address | S.I.G Clothing | A barbing salon |
| Order | One customer, one invoice, one or more Items | One order containing a suit | One appointment |
| Item | One garment or product line inside an Order. **The Item moves through the stages.** | The suit top; the suit trousers | The haircut |
| Stage | A step an Item moves through. Each business defines its own list. | Received, sewing, ironing, packaged, ready, delivered | Booked, in chair, finishing, paid, done |
| Task | A unit of work for one Item in one Stage, assigned to one person | Sew the top (Tunde); sew the trousers (Ada) | Haircut (Sola) |
| Skill | What a worker is able to do. Used to suggest who gets a task. Each tailor has one main skill. | Top sewing, trouser sewing, agbada sewing, ironing | Fade cut, beard shaping |

**Two more ideas that shape the screens.**

- **Item details are flexible.** A tailoring Item carries fabric photos, fabric type and measurements. A farm Item carries crop type, quantity and harvest date. Each business type has its own set of fields on an Item, chosen during setup. Design the order form as a configurable form, not a fixed one.
- **Everything leaves a trail, and the trail cannot be changed.** Every status change, payment and assignment records who did it and when. History is **append-only**: nothing is overwritten or deleted. A correction is a new entry. The order detail screen always shows this history clearly, because it settles disputes about work and pay.

**How an Item moves.**

1. The owner defines the stages once (Phase 1: a fixed tailoring template).
2. When the order is confirmed, each Item enters its first working stage and the platform creates the tasks for that stage only.
3. The platform suggests a worker per task (see §13) and the manager accepts or changes it.
4. Workers update progress. When all tasks for the Item in a stage are **approved**, the Item moves to the next stage and its tasks are created at that moment. This avoids assigning an ironer days before the garment is ready.
5. The Order is **Ready** when all its Items are Ready.
6. Customers are notified at stages the business marks as customer-visible.

**What the customer sees.** The tracking page shows the Order at the stage of its **least advanced Item**, with each Item listed below it.

