## 4. Design principles and constraints

Our users run workshops and shops, not offices. The design has to work in their real conditions: busy hands, cheap phones, patchy internet and little patience for training.

### 4.1 Principles

- **Worker app first for speed.** A tailor should open the app and see their next task within two taps. Large touch targets, one primary action per screen, readable in bright light.
- **Setup in 60 minutes or less.** A non-technical owner should go from sign-up to first saved order in one sitting, measured as a median of 60 minutes or less. Use plain language, sensible defaults and ready-made templates per business type.
- **Warm, not corporate.** These are real people running real businesses. We want human, confident and friendly. **Designer decision:** original creative direction, including illustration, iconography and tone.
- **One screen, one job.** Prefer simple focused screens over dense dashboards. Show what needs attention first (late jobs, unpaid invoices, tasks on hold).
- **Every business feels like its own.** The customer-facing side takes on the business's logo, colors and name. The platform's own brand stays in the background.
- **Money is always clear.** Anywhere an amount appears, show paid, balance and status the same way across the product.

### 4.2 Constraints

| Area | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Devices | Worker app on low and mid-range Android phones first, iOS second. Manager screens on laptop and tablet. Customer pages on any phone browser. Starting values **[CONFIRM against S.I.G's real phones]**: Chrome on Android 8 and up with 2 GB RAM. |
| Worker app type | Phase 1: an **installable web app** (PWA). A native app is a later decision. |
| Connectivity (Phase 1) | A worker can open assigned tasks (with measurements and photos) and change task status with **no internet**. Changes sync when the phone reconnects. If a task was reassigned or cancelled while the worker was offline, the update is **held** and the manager sees it. Wages use the time the **server received** the update. The device time is also stored. Creating orders needs internet. Show clear offline, "waiting to sync" and "synced" states. |
| Connectivity (Phase 4) | Full offline operation for all roles, with conflict handling. |
| Page weight | Customer tracking page loads in under 3 seconds on a 3G connection, with under 200 KB before photos. |
| Photos | Resized on the phone to 1600 px on the long edge before upload. Maximum 10 photos per Item **[CONFIRM]**. Photos taken offline are uploaded when the phone reconnects. |
| Language | English at launch. Layouts must survive longer text so Yoruba, Hausa, Igbo, Nigerian Pidgin and French can be added later. Ask S.I.G's workers which language they read (see §11.2). |
| Currency | Naira first, stored in whole kobo. Currency symbol is a business setting so other currencies work later. |
| Time zone | Stored in UTC. Shown in the business's time zone (default Africa/Lagos). |
| Accessibility | Strong contrast, text that scales, and status never shown by color alone. |
| Branding | Each business can set a logo, one or two brand colors and a name. Define how these flow through customer-facing screens without breaking readability. |
| Responsive | Manager web screens work from tablet width upward. Customer pages work from small phone width upward. |
| Privacy | Customer consent to messages is recorded at order creation. Measurements and photos are visible only to staff of that business. **Saved measurements are kept until the owner or manager updates or deletes them.** Deleting a saved measurement set removes it from the customer's profile only. The copy on a past order stays, because it is part of that order's record for rework and pay. If a customer asks to be erased, the owner erases the customer record. That clears name, phone, address, saved measurements, measurement copies on their orders and photos. The orders, payments and wage records stay and show "Deleted customer". The retention period for photos is a **pre-launch check with a lawyer** under Nigeria's data protection law. |

### 4.3 Front-end requirements

Three items that design, front-end and back-end must agree on. All numbers are starting values **[CONFIRM]**.

#### 4.3.1 Offline interface states

These apply to the worker app. Every state uses **text plus an icon**, never color alone.

| State | When it shows | What the worker sees |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Online | Phone is connected | No marker |
| Offline | No connection | A banner: "You are offline. Your changes will be saved." |
| Waiting to sync | A change was made offline and is queued | A marker on that task, plus a count of waiting changes |
| Syncing | The phone reconnected and is sending changes | A progress marker on the queued items |
| Synced | A queued change was accepted | A short confirmation, then the marker disappears |
| Held | The task was reassigned or cancelled before the change arrived | "Held: your manager changed this task. They will review your update." The worker cannot edit the task until the manager acts. |
| Failed | A change could not be sent after retries | "Could not save." with a **Retry** button. The change is kept on the phone. |

Rules:
- Photos taken offline show their own "waiting to upload" marker.
- The task list and task detail must open with no connection, using the data already on the phone.
- The manager's order timeline shows the phone time and the time the server received the update.
- Creating orders needs internet. Order screens show "Needs internet" when offline.

