## 5. Phase 1: Tailoring MVP

**Goal.** Let one tailoring or clothing business run its entire order cycle on the platform: take the order, assign the sewing, track progress, collect payment, notify the customer and pay staff correctly. Target: a real business like S.I.G Clothing using it daily with 3 to 20 staff. Build for S.I.G. Judge the gate on three pilot businesses.

**Phase 1 is successful when the eight measures in §16.1 are met.**

### 5.1 Features in scope

| Area | What the user can do |
| --- | --- |
| Business setup | Create the business, add logo and colors, choose the tailoring template (stages and fields come pre-filled), set the default payment plan (the manager can change it for each order), the pay period (start day, payday and cut-off time), payment terms and **bank details shown on invoices**, invite staff. The business gets a free subdomain (e.g. `sigclothing.ojuowo.com`) chosen at sign-up. The owner can change the subdomain up to **3 times**; each old address redirects to the current one for 30 days after the change. |
| Business landing page | Each business gets a public page at its subdomain. The owner fills a guided editor (headline, about text, services, how-it-works steps, customer stories, contact details) with a live preview. The page uses the business's logo and brand colors. Changes autosave as a draft; the owner publishes when ready. The page is not live until published. See §5.5 for full details. |
| Staff payouts (Paga) | The owner reviews a weekly wage run and pays all workers to their bank accounts through Paga. The simpler fallback (if Paga integration is not ready) is "mark as paid" with a downloadable payment list. The owner can also set an automatic payout schedule (sweep). Business balances are shown as four buckets: Total, Available, Payout, In transit. See §5.6 for full details. |
| Staff accounts | Owner creates staff by phone number, sets role and skills (top, trousers, agbada, ironing, packaging). Each tailor has one main skill. Owner sets the **pay rate per piece**: one amount for each item type and stage (for example, a set amount for each agbada sewn). Complexity levels are optional. If the owner sets one rate, every Item uses it. Manager can mark a worker as **away** for the day. |
| Customers | Create a customer profile with contact details and message consent. Store measurement sets, style preferences and order history. Reuse saved measurements for returning customers. The owner or manager can edit or delete a saved measurement set at any time. |
| Order intake | Create an order for a customer. Add one or more Items (top, trousers, and so on). For each Item attach fabric photos, material type, color, quality, quantity, design choice, reference images, measurements, due date, price, and **complexity (simple, standard, complex) [CONFIRM levels]**. Measurements are copied onto the Item when the order is confirmed, so later changes to the customer's saved set do not alter past orders. |
| Task assignment | Tasks for a stage are created when an Item enters that stage. The platform suggests a worker per task (skill match, then fewest open tasks). The manager accepts, changes, or assigns manually. |
| Worker queue | Worker sees only their assigned tasks, with measurements and fabric photos attached. Updates status (see §5.4). Can work offline (see §4.2). |
| Stage hand-off | When all sewing tasks on an Item are approved, the Item moves to ironing and the ironer sees it. When ironing is done, the Item moves to packaging, then Ready. |
| Verification | Each stage has a setting **"Needs manager approval"**. Tailoring template: sewing **yes**; ironing and packaging **no** (auto-approved when completed). The manager approves or sends back for rework with a note. Only approved or auto-approved tasks count toward wages. |
| Invoicing | Generate an invoice from the order. Every invoice has a **due date** (default: the order due date, changeable under payment terms). A sent invoice is **locked**. A change creates a new numbered revision, and the old one is marked superseded. Send by WhatsApp with the tracking link. The invoice shows the business's bank details for transfer. Shows the order's payment plan and the balance still owed. |
| Payments | **Customers do not pay through the platform.** They pay the business directly by cash or transfer. The manager records each payment. **Payment plan is chosen for each order:** pay in full first; part now and the rest when the job is done; or several part payments. The business default pre-fills the choice. If the plan needs money first (full or part), the order waits at Confirmed until that amount is recorded. The manager can start anyway by giving a reason. Every payment is stored as whole kobo, with who and when, and **cannot be deleted**. A mistake is fixed with a reversing entry. Final receipt when fully paid. |
| Cancel order | Manager cancels an order with a required reason. If money was paid, the manager records a **manual refund entry**. There are no online refunds, because customers do not pay online. |
| Notifications | Exactly the Phase 1 rows in §10.1. |
| Customer tracking page | A branded page, opened from a WhatsApp link with no login, showing the Order stage, each Item's stage, balance and pickup or delivery details. |
| Wages | **Pay is per piece.** Each approved task is logged against the worker with its pay amount. A worker's pay for a pay period is the total of their approved tasks (for example, the number of agbada sewn times the price for each agbada). Records of every task are kept. The pay period is a business setting (default: Monday start, **Saturday-night** payday). The owner can also run a wage run for **any date range**. The owner reviews, can add **adjustment lines** (amount and reason), and marks the run as paid. Workers see their own earnings. |
| Dashboard and reports | Dashboard: today's orders, late orders, unpaid balances, tasks on hold. Reports: late orders, unpaid balances, weekly revenue, wage summary. |

### 5.2 Not in Phase 1

- Other business types and the workflow configurator (Phase 2A).
- **Custom domains** (Phase 2B). Phase 1 uses the free subdomain on our address only.
- **Basic inventory** (moved to Phase 2C, together with deeper inventory).
- The **supplier network** (Phase 3).
- Subcontractors (Phase 2B), route optimization and delivery partner integration (Phase 3).
- Native customer app. Customers use WhatsApp and the web page.
- **Customers paying through the platform**, and online refunds. Payments are recorded by the manager.
- **Subscription billing** (Phase 2A).
- **Discounts and tax on invoices.** The manager enters the final price.
- **Delivery handled by the platform.** The business arranges delivery. The order only records "delivered".
- **Replies to WhatsApp messages.** They are not handled in Phase 1. Messages say: "This number is not monitored. Call [business phone]."
- Multiple workers on one task (one active assignee per task).

### 5.3 The order journey

This is the main flow to design first. Each step lists who does it and what the platform does.

1. **Customer walks in.** The manager finds or creates the customer. If the customer is returning, saved measurements appear.
2. **Manager creates the order.** Adds each Item, uploads fabric photos, fills material details, confirms measurements, sets complexity, price and due date. Customer consent to messages is recorded.
3. **Invoice and payment plan.** Platform generates an invoice. The manager picks the payment plan for this order (the business default pre-fills it): full first, part now and balance later, or several part payments. The customer pays the business by cash or transfer, and the manager records it. If the plan needs money first, the order stays **Confirmed but not started** until that amount is recorded. The manager can start anyway by giving a reason.
4. **Tasks are created and assigned.** Sewing tasks are created for each Item. The top goes to one tailor, the trousers to another. Platform suggests, manager confirms.
5. **Workers sew.** Each worker opens their queue, sees the fabric photos and measurements, starts the task, updates status, completes it.
6. **Manager verifies quality.** Approves or sends back for rework with a note. The same worker redoes the work. Approval is what moves the Item to ironing.
7. **Ironing and packaging.** The ironer sees the Item, completes the ironing task, then the packaging task. Both are auto-approved on completion.
8. **Customer is notified.** WhatsApp message: your clothes are ready, with balance due and pickup or delivery details.
9. **Final payment and release.** Customer pays the balance to the business, in person or by transfer. The manager records it and the platform issues the receipt. Manager marks the order collected or delivered. If a balance remains, the manager must give a reason to release it.
10. **Wages log.** Each approved task is added to the worker's total for the current pay period.

*Phase 1 order journey: 10 steps, 1 rework loop.* Only approved tasks (step 6, or auto-approved in step 7) are logged toward wages in step 10.

**Wage rules**

- The task's **pay amount is copied onto the task when it is approved.** A later rate change never rewrites past pay.
- **Pay is per piece.** A worker's amount for a task is the rate for that item type and stage. The more approved pieces, the higher the pay.
- The **pay period is a business setting**: start day (default Monday), payday (default Saturday) and cut-off time (starting value: Saturday 6 pm). Work approved after the cut-off goes into the next run. Sunday work goes into the next run by default, and the owner can move it into the current one.
- The owner can run a wage run for **any date range**, for example when a week has many jobs or payday moves. A task can be in only one wage run.
- **Rework** is done by the same worker and paid **once**, when finally approved.
- If a task is **reassigned after work has started**, the manager decides how much the first worker is paid and records it as an adjustment line with a reason.
- The owner can add an **adjustment line** (amount and reason) to any wage run. Adjustments are recorded and cannot be deleted.
- Wages use the time the server received an update, not the phone's clock.

### 5.4 Screens and states that need special attention

- **Order form.** Long and detailed, so design it in steps or sections. Photo upload from the phone camera must be fast. Returning-customer measurements should pre-fill.
- **Measurement sheet.** Needs a clear, human layout (body diagram or labeled fields). **Designer decision:** how to make measurements quick to enter and easy to read at a sewing table.
- **Worker task card.** Must show the Item, who it is for (first name), due date, fabric photo, measurements and one big status action.
- **Order timeline.** A vertical history of every stage, task, payment and note. Used by the manager and, in simplified form, by the customer.
- **Empty states.** A brand-new business has no orders or staff. Empty screens should guide the next step.

**One list of states, used everywhere.**

| Object | States | Allowed moves |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Task | Assigned, In progress, On hold (reason required), Completed, Needs rework, Approved | Assigned → In progress. In progress → On hold or Completed. On hold → In progress. Completed → Approved or Needs rework. Needs rework → In progress. |
| Order | Draft, Confirmed, In progress, Ready, Collected or Delivered, Cancelled | Draft → Confirmed → In progress → Ready → Collected or Delivered. Cancel allowed from Draft, Confirmed or In progress. |
| Payment (of an order) | Unpaid, Part paid, Paid, Overdue, Refunded | **Overdue** is not stored. It means the invoice due date has passed and a balance remains. **Refunded** means a manual refund entry was recorded. |

- An order is **Late** when its due date has passed and it is not Ready (business time zone).
- Putting a task **On hold** notifies the manager and shows on the dashboard.
- Allowed moves are defined in one table and enforced on the server. They also decide what happens in a sync conflict.

### 5.5 Business landing page

Every business gets a public page at its subdomain. The owner never edits layout or code — we control the template. The owner supplies text, images and colors.

**Owner flow**

1. Open "My page" from the dashboard or the onboarding checklist.
2. The page opens pre-filled with example content for a tailoring business; the owner edits instead of facing a blank page.
3. Fill sections in a guided editor with a live preview (phone and laptop toggle).
4. Autosaves as a draft. Nothing is public until the owner publishes.
5. Preview via a private link.
6. Publish. The page goes live at the business's subdomain.
7. Share: copy link, show QR code, or tap to send on WhatsApp.
8. Edit later: changes stay as a draft until the owner publishes again. The owner can unpublish.

**Sections the owner fills**

| Section | What they provide | Required |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Header | Logo (from setup) and business name. Nav links are automatic. | Yes |
| Hero | Headline (60 chars), supporting line (140 chars), one main image, button action (call, WhatsApp, or request an order). | Yes |
| About | Heading, text (600 chars), optional image. | Yes |
| What we offer | Up to 12 items — image, title (40 chars), description (140 chars), optional "from" price. At least 1. | Yes |
| How it works | 3–5 numbered steps. Title 30 chars, text 100 chars. | No |
| Customer stories | Up to 6 quotes — name, quote (200 chars), optional photo. | No |
| Contact | Phone, WhatsApp, address, opening hours, map link, social links. At least one contact method. | Yes |
| Footer | Automatic: business name, links, "Made with Ojúòwò" mark. | Automatic |

**Look and colors**

| Setting | Options |
| --- | --- |
| Primary color | Swatches or typed color code. Used for buttons and highlights. |
| Accent color | Swatches or typed color code. Used sparingly. |
| Font style | A small set of pairings chosen by the designer (3 or 4). No free font upload. |
| Corner style | Sharp, soft, or round. Applies to buttons and cards. |
| Hero style | Image with overlay, or solid color block. Text always stays readable. |

Automatic readability check: if colors make text unreadable, the page warns and offers the closest readable alternative. The owner cannot publish unreadable text.

**Page states to design:** not started, draft, published, published with unpublished changes, unpublished.

### 5.6 Staff payouts, business balances, and automatic sweep

**Overview.** The owner reviews a wage run and pays all workers to their bank accounts through Paga. Ojúòwò never holds the money. Payouts depend on confirmation from the lawyer and Paga. Until both confirm, the safe fallback is: the owner approves the run, the system produces a downloadable payment list, and the owner marks each person paid after paying outside the system. Design the flow so the live Paga payout step can be switched on later without redesigning the rest.

**Wage run flow**

1. Open Pay and choose the period (default: current week).
2. Review the wage run: each worker with approved tasks, rate per task, and total. Salaried staff show a fixed amount.
3. Open any worker to see the tasks behind their total. Tasks awaiting approval are shown separately and not counted.
4. Adjust: add a bonus or deduction with a required reason. Each adjustment is recorded with who made it.
5. Resolve warnings: worker with no bank details, task with no rate, total much higher than usual.
6. Approve the run. After approval the run is locked; rates are fixed as they were at approval.
7. Pay: owner confirms, enters PIN or one-time code. System shows total and recipient count.
8. Watch progress: each person shows as sending, paid, or failed.
9. Handle failures: owner sees the reason, fixes bank details, retries only that person.
10. Each worker gets a WhatsApp/SMS notification and sees the payment in their earnings.

**Worker earnings view.** The worker sees: earnings to date this week, approved tasks behind it, tasks waiting for approval (not counted yet), and payment history. If they think a task was missed, they can raise a question that goes to the manager.

**Business balances (Paga account)**

Each business has one account at Paga. We show it as four named balances. Moving money between them is instant and does not call Paga.

| Balance | Meaning | Money in | Money out |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Total | Sum of all other balances. Read-only. | — | — |
| Available | Money the business can spend or withdraw. | Customer payments, top-ups from the owner's bank. | Withdraw to bank, move to Payout, pay suppliers (later). |
| Payout | Money set aside for staff pay only. | Moved from Available, or topped up directly. | Staff payouts only. Can be moved back to Available by the owner. |
| In transit | Payout money sent and not yet confirmed. | Created when a payout starts. | Clears to paid or returns to Payout on failure. |

Rules:
- Wage payouts come only from the Payout balance. If short, the owner is told by how much before sending.
- Moving money into Payout needs a manager or owner. Moving it back out needs the owner.
- Customer payments land in Available, not automatically in Payout. An optional rule can set aside a percentage.
- Unregistered businesses can use Available and Payout only after reaching Paga KYC level 3. Show a progress screen until then.

**Automatic payout sweep**

The owner or a manager can set a schedule (e.g. every Friday at 4 pm). On that day the system pays each worker their approved earnings from the Payout balance.

Rules:
- The sweep pays only approved tasks; each task is paid once.
- The owner sets a limit per sweep and per person. Anything above the limit waits for owner approval.
- A manager can create a sweep, but it starts only after the owner approves the schedule and limit once.
- If Payout balance is short, the sweep pays nobody and notifies the owner. An option lets the sweep top up from Available up to a set amount.
- If a worker changed their bank details in the last 24 hours, their payment waits for owner confirmation.
- Each sweep produces a summary: who was paid, who failed, and why.
- The owner can pause all sweeps with one switch.
- The manual wage run in §5.3 remains available for owners who want to review before paying.

**Safeguards to show in the UI**
- Pay always needs the owner's approval, never a manager's alone (unless the owner turns on delegation).
- A confirmation step shows the total and recipient count before money moves.
- A payment cannot be sent twice. A duplicate tap shows the first result only.
- The business needs enough Payout balance before sending. If not, the screen says so and tells the owner what to do.
- Every step has a record of who did it and when.

**States to design:** no approved work this period, run in draft, run approved, payout in progress, partly paid, all paid, person failed (with reason), run waiting on missing bank details, provider unavailable, insufficient Payout balance, and a run corrected after the fact (new adjustment in the next run, never an edit of a closed run).

