## 6. Phase 2: Many business types, customization and custom domains

**Goal.** Turn the tailoring product into a platform. Any stage-based business can sign up, describe how it works, and get a system shaped to it, with its own branding, public page and web address.

Phase 2 is split into three parts, built and gated in order.

### 6.1 Phase 2A: Templates, workflow, branding, plans

| Area | What the user can do |
| --- | --- |
| Business type onboarding | During sign-up the owner picks a business type (tailoring, shoe making, farm selling by order, other). The platform loads a matching template of stages, order fields, roles and terms. |
| Workflow builder | Owner adds, removes, renames and reorders stages. For each stage choose who does it (role or skill), whether it creates tasks, whether it needs manager approval, and whether the customer is notified. |
| Custom order fields | Owner adds or removes fields on an Item and on a customer profile (text, number, date, photo, dropdown, measurement set). |
| Custom labels | Rename platform words (worker becomes stylist, order becomes appointment). |
| Branding | Upload logo, choose brand colors and font style from a small set, preview on customer screens before publishing. |
| Customer online payments and refunds | Not planned. Added only if the business decides customers should pay through the platform. |
| Plans and billing | Business picks a subscription plan, upgrades or downgrades, sees invoices from us and plan limits. The subscription is paid through Paystack (card or bank transfer). |

**Rules for live businesses**

- Editing a workflow changes **only new Items**. Items already in progress keep their stage.
- A stage that has Items in it can be **hidden but not deleted**.
- Removing a field **hides it and keeps its data**.

### 6.2 Phase 2B: Public presence, customers, appointments, subcontractors

| Area | What the user can do |
| --- | --- |
| Public landing page | **Moved to Phase 1** (see §5.5). Every business gets a branded landing page from day one. |
| Custom domain | Business has a free subdomain from Phase 1. Phase 2B adds the ability to connect the business's own domain, with a guided setup that checks the DNS connection and shows clear status, including wrong or expired domain states. |
| Customer onboarding | Customers request an order from the landing page, create an optional account, see all their orders. |
| Appointments | For service businesses like salons: bookable time slots, per-staff schedules, reminders and protection against double booking. This is a **new module**, separate from the stage model. |
| Subcontractors | A manager assigns a task to an external person with a limited login. They see only that task and update its progress. |
| Skill-based auto-assignment | A manager can switch on automatic assignment by skill and workload, with manual override. |

### 6.3 Phase 2C: Depth

| Area | What the user can do |
| --- | --- |
| Inventory | Record materials (fabric, thread, buttons) with quantity, color and supplier name. Low-stock alerts. Stock used per order. Then stock movements, purchase records and multiple storage locations. |
| Expenses | Record running costs such as fuel, generator servicing and equipment maintenance, and see them against revenue. |
| Two-way WhatsApp | Customers can reply and the business can answer inside the platform. Until then, replies are directed to the business's own phone number. |

### 6.4 Flows to design

- **New business sign-up and setup wizard.** Account, business type, template preview, branding, staff invite, first order. Target 60 minutes or less.
- **Workflow builder.** Visual and forgiving: drag stages around and see a live preview. **Designer decision:** the interaction model for building a workflow without it feeling technical.
- **Landing page editor.** Choose blocks, add photos, preview on phone and laptop, publish.
- **Custom domain connection.** Step-by-step, copyable values, a "check my connection" button, and clear success and failure states.
- **Customer request flow.** From the landing page to a submitted request to confirmation, in as few taps as possible.
- **Plan selection and upgrade.** Plans compared simply, with limits shown where they matter (for example when a business tries to add an eleventh staff member on a plan that allows ten).

### 6.5 Design challenge

The same screens now serve very different businesses. The design system must stretch from a sewing workshop to a farm to a salon through configurable fields, labels, colors and stage lists, without any of them feeling like a generic template.

