## 7. Phase 3: Payroll, suppliers, logistics and analytics

**Goal.** Pay staff through the platform, connect businesses to the people they buy from, and give owners deeper insight.

**Build order: payroll first, then the supplier network.** Payroll uses existing users. Suppliers require recruiting a second kind of user.

### 7.1 Features in scope

| Area | What the user can do |
| --- | --- |
| Staff payroll payouts | **Moved to Phase 1** via Paga (see §5.6). Phase 3 extends this with platform commission on each payout (rate **[CONFIRM]**), deeper payslip history, and advances/deductions. |
| Business wallet | The Paga-backed balance model is in Phase 1 (see §5.6). Phase 3 adds subscription payments from the wallet and a commission on payouts, subject to legal review. |
| Payroll safeguards | Worker account-name verification before the first payout. Per-worker success or failure result. **Payouts are not assumed to arrive on Sundays.** The screen shows the expected arrival time and warns when a payout would land on a Sunday **[CONFIRM with Paga]**. |
| Advances and deductions | Record salary advances, penalties and bonuses and see them in the wage run. |
| Supplier accounts | A supplier creates a shop profile, lists items with prices and photos, and manages stock. |
| Business to supplier link | A business invites its regular suppliers. A supplier can connect with many businesses. Invite-only. |
| Procurement | Business creates a purchase request, the supplier accepts, prices are confirmed, the business pays through the platform and goods are marked received. Stock updates automatically. |
| Supplier payment requests | A supplier can request payment **only against a purchase request the business accepted**. The business approves and pays. |
| Supplier dashboard | Supplier sees orders from connected businesses, who has paid and who owes, and stock levels. |
| Delivery requests | Create a delivery request with a third-party partner, share tracking with the customer and confirm drop-off. |
| Route planning | Show pending deliveries on a map and suggest an efficient route. |
| Equipment and maintenance | List equipment, schedule servicing, log fuel and repairs, and get reminders. |
| Analytics | Revenue trends, high-value customers, popular designs and materials, average payment time, worker productivity, average completion time, inventory usage, and cost against revenue. |
| Exports | Download reports as PDF or spreadsheet for accountants. |

### 7.2 Flows to design

- **Weekly payroll run.** Review totals per worker, adjust, confirm, pay, and view the receipt. High-trust moment, so make amounts and confirmations unmistakable.
- **Connecting to a supplier.** Invite by phone or link, accept, see each other's profile.
- **Buying materials.** From browsing a supplier's items to placing a request, paying and confirming receipt.
- **Supplier sends a payment request.** How it appears for the business, and how approval and payment feel.
- **Delivery handoff.** Request, partner accepted, in transit, delivered, with the customer kept informed.
- **Owner analytics view.** **Designer decision:** how to present a lot of numbers so an owner who is not a data person can act on them.

### 7.3 Design challenge

A second kind of user, the supplier, now has their own small business on the platform. Design their experience as a lighter sibling of the business owner's experience, and make the connection feel like a trusted relationship, not a marketplace of strangers.

**Data rule.** A supplier connection crosses business boundaries. Each side sees only the fields on shared orders and nothing else.

