## 12. Technical requirements

Stack: **Next.js and TypeScript**, Prisma, PostgreSQL. Product choices for hosting, queues and storage are made by engineering. The requirements below are what the product needs.

### 12.1 Multi-tenancy

- Every table row carries its business. Every query filters by business through one shared helper.
- A second line of defence at the database level (row-level security) prevents one business from reading another's data.
- Tenant is resolved from the subdomain (`{slug}.[platform domain]`). Reserved names (such as admin, app, api, www) cannot be used as slugs.
- Supplier connections (Phase 3) use an explicit Connection record. Each side sees only the fields on shared orders.

### 12.2 Worker app (PWA)

- Installable web app with a service worker.
- Local store for the worker's assigned tasks, measurements and photos.
- An action queue for status updates and photos taken offline, sent in order when the phone reconnects.
- Each queued update carries a unique id, so a retry never creates a duplicate.
- Conflict rule: if a task was reassigned or cancelled before the update arrives, the update is held for manager review.
- Push notifications on Android. iOS web push is limited, so iOS relies on in-app and WhatsApp.

### 12.3 Money

**Phase 1.** Customers do not pay through the platform. The manager records payments received by the business (cash or transfer).

- All amounts are stored as whole kobo in a 64-bit integer. Never as decimals.
- Payments are append-only. Mistakes are corrected with a reversing entry.
- Manager-recorded payments store who and when. The owner can see them.
- Sent invoices are locked. A change creates a new numbered revision.
- Invoices show the business's bank details so customers can pay by transfer.

**Business balances (Phase 1, via Paga).** Each business has one Paga account represented as four named balances (Total, Available, Payout, In transit). See §5.6 for full rules. Provider: **Paga**.

- Staff payouts go from the Payout balance to workers' bank accounts.
- Top-ups and payouts use signature-checked webhooks that are safe to receive twice, keyed on the provider's reference.
- Each payout shows the total and recipient count before the owner confirms.
- Payouts are not assumed to arrive on Sundays. Show the expected arrival time and warn on Sundays **[CONFIRM with Paga]**.
- Balance entries are append-only. A nightly job compares Paga records to our entries and flags differences.
- Unregistered businesses require KYC level 3 on Paga before payouts are enabled.
- Fallback (until Paga integration is confirmed): "mark as paid" with a downloadable payment list. Design must support both paths without redesigning the flow.

**Business wallet extended (Phase 3).** Phase 3 adds subscription billing from the wallet and a commission on each payout (rate **[CONFIRM]**), shown to the owner before they confirm. Subject to legal review.

### 12.4 Messaging

- Messages go through a queue with retries.
- Delivery status comes back from the provider and is stored per message.
- Failed messages show to the manager.
- One platform sender number in Phase 1.

### 12.5 Security

- Tracking tokens have at least 128 bits of randomness and can be regenerated.
- PINs are stored hashed. One-time codes expire and allow a limited number of attempts. Both are rate limited.
- All traffic uses HTTPS.
- Permissions are checked on the server on every request, using the matrix in §2.2.
- History tables are append-only. No update or delete is exposed in the application.

### 12.6 Performance and reliability (starting values, [CONFIRM])

- Tracking page: under 3 seconds on 3G and under 200 KB before photos.
- Photos resized on the phone before upload, then stored in object storage.
- Availability target: 99.5% per month.
- Daily backups with a **tested restore**. Recovery point 24 hours, recovery time 4 hours.
- Database connections are pooled so serverless functions do not exhaust them.

### 12.7 Measurement

Events are recorded from day one so the gates in §16 can be reported: setup start and finish, who changed each task, message delivery status, wage run edits, and tracking page opens.

